[
{"insertText":{"location":{"index":144924,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"text":"\nAt the frozen source thresholds, however, SwinFusion++ did not have the highest transfer accuracy, recall, or F1-score in any direction (point estimates; several margins were one or two videos), and in the supplementary paired intervals its transfer accuracy was lower than an encoder’s in 6 of 18 comparisons and higher in 2 (Table J2). This limitation is shared by all three models and follows from the frozen-transfer design: with thresholds set on each training source’s score distribution, every model flagged fewer target videos as fake than the target set contained, which kept specificity at 91% or higher but lowered recall. ROC-AUC measures the ranking of fake above real videos without depending on that threshold, which is why ROC-AUC is the primary measure in this study (Chapter 3); in practice, a detector used on a new kind of video needs its operating point re-established with labeled examples from that setting."}},
{"updateParagraphStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":144925,"endIndex":145856,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"paragraphStyle":{"namedStyleType":"NORMAL_TEXT","alignment":"JUSTIFIED","lineSpacing":200,"indentFirstLine":{"magnitude":36,"unit":"PT"},"spaceAbove":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"spaceBelow":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"keepWithNext":false},"fields":"namedStyleType,alignment,lineSpacing,indentFirstLine,spaceAbove,spaceBelow,keepWithNext"}},
{"updateTextStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":144925,"endIndex":145855,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"textStyle":{"fontSize":{"magnitude":12,"unit":"PT"}},"fields":"bold,italic,underline,backgroundColor,fontSize"}},
{"updateTextStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":145855,"endIndex":145856,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"textStyle":{"fontSize":{"magnitude":12,"unit":"PT"}},"fields":"bold,italic,underline,backgroundColor,fontSize"}},
{"insertText":{"location":{"index":144924,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"text":"\nUnder frozen cross-dataset evaluation, SwinFusion++ had significantly higher ROC-AUC than Swin-B in seven of the nine directions (by +0.049 to +0.258) and than Video Swin-B in three (by +0.063 to +0.084), and in those three it was significantly better than both encoders. It was not significantly lower than either encoder in any direction, although the widest interval reached −0.092, and nine of the ten significant differences had the same sign in every pairing of individual seeds. The advantage came mainly from the Celeb-DF v2 and DeeperForensics-1.0 sources; from FF++ C23, the one detectable difference was also the largest, +0.258 against Swin-B on DeeperForensics-1.0. This is the setting that motivated SwinFusion++, which combines frame appearance with temporal evidence: detectors that exploit temporal information, such as mouth movements or temporal coherence, have been reported to perform better on unseen manipulations and datasets than frame-based detectors (Haliassos et al., 2021; Zheng et al., 2021). The present results are consistent with that account, while the attribution of the gain to TLBG-HCA itself is tested under RQ4."}},
{"updateParagraphStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":144925,"endIndex":146076,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"paragraphStyle":{"namedStyleType":"NORMAL_TEXT","alignment":"JUSTIFIED","lineSpacing":200,"indentFirstLine":{"magnitude":36,"unit":"PT"},"spaceAbove":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"spaceBelow":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"keepWithNext":false},"fields":"namedStyleType,alignment,lineSpacing,indentFirstLine,spaceAbove,spaceBelow,keepWithNext"}},
