{"updateTextStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":146075,"endIndex":146076,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"textStyle":{"fontSize":{"magnitude":12,"unit":"PT"}},"fields":"bold,italic,underline,backgroundColor,fontSize"}},
{"insertText":{"location":{"index":144924,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"text":"\nIn distribution, under the principal fixed-recipe comparison, SwinFusion++ performed at the same near-ceiling level as standalone Swin-B and Video Swin-B. All three models reached ROC-AUC of 0.988 to 0.997, no paired ROC-AUC difference was detectable, and each paired interval lay between −0.011 and +0.009 AUC; at this ceiling, little room remained for a detectable improvement. The threshold-dependent metrics were similarly close: accuracy differed by at most three videos on FaceForensics++, and precision and recall differed from those of either encoder by at most 2.0 and 3.1 percentage points. The only supplementary accuracy and F1-score intervals that excluded zero favored SwinFusion++ (Celeb-DF v2 against Swin-B; DeeperForensics-1.0 against Video Swin-B); because the ROC-AUC differences there were not detectable, these gains are best read as threshold placement rather than better ranking. This near-ceiling pattern agrees with the benchmark literature, in which strong detectors reach very high accuracy when training and test videos come from the same benchmark (Rössler et al., 2019), whereas detectors trained on one dataset perform much worse on a dataset they have not seen (Li et al., 2020)."}},
